messages:
Anya: Quick question from the console review: in the data-table modal the primary CTA is right-aligned and looks cramped when toasts appear stacked under the action row. debating options: center primary for confirm modals, or switch to vertical action stacking at narrow widths. thoughts? https://figma.redwood/console/modals :thinking: @Mike @Julia
Mike: vertical stack for narrow widths +1. Centering primary for all modals will clash with left-aligned table content. Prefer: keep left-aligned for content-heavy modals, center-only for tiny confirm dialogs (delete, overwrite).
Julia: Agreed. Also noticed spacing token feels tight — currently using `surface-gap-sm` (~8px). With toast shadow it visually collapses. Recommend bump to 12px and add a `compact` alias. I'll draft token proposal.
Kevin: Stack on <480px breakpoints makes sense. For implementation, here's a small CSS snippet we can reuse in storybook:
```
.modal .action-row {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row;
  gap: var(--space-2);
}
@media (max-width: 480px) {
  .modal .action-row { flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch; }
}
```
Also flag: ensure toasts don't capture focus in a way that prevents keyboard users from reaching modal actions.
Anya: thanks — decision: console/data modals = left-aligned actions; confirm dialogs = centered; responsive stack below 480px. I'll update Figma and add spec notes. @design-bot can you run a contrast and focus-order check for the toast CTA? The visual looked low-contrast.
design-bot: Contrast scan result: toast CTA label contrast ratio = 3.6:1 vs toast bg (fails AA for 14px normal). Recommendations: use `text-on-surface-high` or increase weight to 600. Focus order check: when toast appears while modal is open, ensure aria-live does not steal focus and that tab order remains modal -> toast only if user navigates. Full report: https://internal-reports/design/contrast/1715559123
Julia: I'll open a token PR: rename `surface-gap-sm` -> `surface-gap-compact` with new value 12, preserve legacy alias for one release. I'll also add an a11y checklist item about toast focus management and add a Storybook interaction test for keyboard navigation when toasts appear.
Mike: Sounds good. Who will own QA for the change? I'll take Storybook tests, Julia can own token PR review, Anya will update Figma and docs. :thumbsup:
Kevin: I'll run a quick visual QA across breakpoints and check the toast stacking on small widths.
Anya: great — thanks everyone. I'll track the tasks in the design ticket and ping when Figma is updated.
